    "note_en": "A plural names more than one thing. Due, tre and quattro mean “two”, “three” and “four”: due sedie means “two chairs”, tre libri “three books”, quattro persone “four people”. An Italian noun changes its ending for number. Here sedia becomes sedie and libro becomes libri. With the definite article, il libro (“the book”) becomes i libri (“the books”); la sedia (“the chair”) becomes le sedie (“the chairs”). Persona is feminine: una persona (“a person”), le persone (“the people”), regardless of that person's gender. These are common patterns, not a guarantee for every Italian noun.\n\nAdjectives agree with their nouns in gender and number. La sedia nera means “the black chair”; le sedie nere means “the black chairs”. Il libro nuovo means “the new book”; i libri nuovi means “the new books”. Vecchia describes one old chair; vecchie describes several old chairs. Bianco/bianca mean “white”; bianchi is the masculine plural. The h before i keeps the hard c sound. Blu keeps the same form. Grande becomes grandi for either gender. Learn each pattern rather than attaching the noun's ending to every adjective.\n\nÈ means “is”; sono here means “are”, with more than one person or thing: una persona è seduta means “one person is seated”; due persone sono sedute means “two people are seated”. Ha means “has”; hanno means “have”: una persona ha un libro means “one person has a book”; due persone hanno i libri means “two people have the books”. These are third-person forms, not yet “I am” or “you have”. Seduto is a descriptive adjective meaning “seated”, not a model for building a past tense. Sicura means “safe/steady” here: an old chair can still be safe. Manca una sedia means “one chair is missing/needed”. The chapter retains only this bounded singular form; it does not teach a general action-verb plural paradigm.\n\nGli is the plural article in gli scaffali, “the shelves”. Its singular article and the general distribution of article variants come next chapter. Anche means “also/too”; ancora means “still” when a situation continues, or “one more” when another chair is brought. In la persona entra ancora, it means “again”: “the person enters again”. Context distinguishes those readings. The story moves books to the shelves to make the chairs usable; it leaves Ada's envelope sealed."
  },
